{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfisWky5odMmGkW998Q5D","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The repaired candidate passes the self-check: it prints `OK`, with a finite ELPD-LOO of −2099.2 on the short smoke fit (one chain, 100 draws). That run doesn't test novelty, convergence or speed, and novelty is the gate that rejected the last attempt.\n\n**Why it was rejected:** the previous attempt made the trick coin favour heads. That is the same idea as the existing `heads_rigged_trick_coin_suspicion`, so the two predicted almost identically (RMSE 0.00018, below the 0.002 limit).\n\n**What I did instead:** I kept the incumbent, `person_specific_motif_suspicion`, and made one different change: people's model of a fair coin now remembers the last flip-to-flip change. Right after a switch, the believed odds of switching again shift by one fitted amount shared by everyone, `d_switch2` (prior Normal(0, 1)). In the model this adds a term for each sequence: half of `d_switch2` times (switches that follow a switch − repeats that follow a switch). If `d_switch2` comes out negative, sequences that keep switching look less random than their total switch count alone implies. This targets the critique that people favour extra switches in already highly alternating pairs less than the incumbent predicts. That critique is only borderline (raw p = 0.05, q = 0.10, so it doesn't survive the FDR).\n\n**Risk:** if the fit puts `d_switch2` near zero, the model predicts like the incumbent and could fail novelty again. There is no further repair after this one. I didn't inspect the fitted value of `d_switch2`.\n\nFiles are in `iter_2/candidate_3_repair_1/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt` — `second_order_chance_motif_suspicion`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2769,"cache_read_input_tokens":85167,"c